.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FOPEN 3S
.SH NAME
fopen, freopen, fdopen, tmpfile, tmpnam, remove, rename \- open, name and remove
a stream's file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *type);
.PP
.B FILE *freopen(const char *filename, const char *type, FILE *stream);
.PP
.B FILE *fdopen(int fildes, const char *type);
.PP
.B FILE *tmpfile(void);
.PP
.B char *tmpnam(char *s);
.PP
.B int remove(const char *filename);
.PP
.B int rename(const char *from, const char *to);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fopen
opens the file named by
.I filename
and associates a stream with it.
.I Fopen
returns a pointer to be used to identify
the stream in subsequent operations.
.PP
.I Type
is a character string having one of the following values:
.TP 5
"r"
open for reading
.ns
.TP 5
"w"
create for writing
.ns
.TP 5
"a"
append: open for writing at end
of file, or create for writing
.PP
.B Note:
a `+' may follow any of the three, opening the stream for update.
.B "w+"
and
.B "a+"
are created and then reopened, because
.IR creat (2)
leaves the descriptor write-only.  A `b' is accepted and ignored: there are no
text and binary streams here, only bytes.
.PP
.I Freopen
substitutes the named file in place
of the open
.IR stream .
It returns the original value of
.IR stream .
The original stream is closed.
.PP
.I Freopen
is typically used to attach the preopened
constant names,
.B stdin, stdout, stderr,
to specified files.
.PP
.I Fdopen
associates a stream with a file descriptor obtained from
.I open, dup, creat,
or
.IR pipe (2).
The
.I type
of the stream must agree with the mode of the open file.
.PP
.B Note:
the four that follow are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.
.PP
.I Tmpfile
creates a temporary file open for update and returns a stream on it.  The name is
.I unlinked
as soon as the stream holds the descriptor, so the file has no name from then on
and the kernel frees the inode when the last reference goes \- which is either
.IR fclose (3)
or the cleanup that
.IR exit (3)
runs.  Nothing is left behind even if the program dies on a signal.
.PP
.I Tmpnam
generates a file name that no existing file has, in a buffer of at least
.B L_tmpnam
characters; a null
.I s
asks for a static buffer that the next call overwrites.  It can distinguish
.B TMP_MAX
names.
.PP
.B Note:
.I tmpnam
does
.I not
go through
.IR mktemp (3).
That routine fills its trailing X's from the process id and then walks a
.I single
letter, so it can distinguish 26 names where TMP_MAX promises 26**3.
.I Tmpnam
counts three letters of its own, and the pid still separates one process's names
from another's.
.PP
.I Remove
deletes a file;
.I rename
changes its name.  Both return 0 on success and a non-zero value on failure, and
both are thin wrappers over
.IR unlink (2)
and
.IR link (2)/ unlink (2).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2),
fclose(3),
setbuf(3),
mktemp(3),
stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fopen,
.I freopen,
.I fdopen
and
.I tmpfile
return the pointer
.SM
.B NULL
if
.I filename
cannot be accessed.
.I Tmpnam
returns NULL if it cannot find an unused name.
.SH BUGS
.I Fdopen
is not portable to systems other than UNIX.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
FOPEN_MAX is _NFILE, which is also NOFILE in <sys/param.h>: one
.B FILE
per descriptor and no more.
.PP
.B There is no path-length limit in this kernel.
.IR Namei ()
walks a component at a time and only the component is bounded, by DIRSIZ.  So
FILENAME_MAX is a convention for sizing a caller's buffer and not a kernel
number; it is v7's customary 128.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/fopen.c, freopen.c, fdopen.c, endopen.c, findiop.c, tmpfile.c,
tmpnam.c, remove.c, rename.c
